// KNOWN FAILURE (wasm backend only): a short-circuit boolean condition
// (`&&` or `||`) used as an if/else condition INSIDE A LOOP, where BOTH
// branches reassign a `str`-typed mutable local via concatenation, makes
// `kryos build --backend wasm` emit a structurally INVALID wasm module.
// The compiler's own post-codegen `wasmparser` validator catches this and
// REFUSES to write the file (a compile-time ICE, not a silent miscompile --
// see docs/wasm-contract.md's "never a miscompile" note for why that
// distinction matters and what it does/doesn't guarantee):
//
//   error: codegen (wasm) failed: wasm codegen: internal: the WASM backend
//   produced a structurally INVALID module and refused to write it (type
//   mismatch: expected i64 but nothing on stack (at offset 0x...))
//
// Found 2026-08-17 while wiring examples/showcase/wordscope.kry's WASM leg
// into tests/run_examples_e2e.sh -- wordscope.kry's `to_lower_ascii` helper
// has exactly this shape (`if c >= 65 && c <= 90 { out = out + tbl[..] }
// else { out = out + substr(..) }` inside a `while` loop) and fails to
// build for wasm as a direct result. Isolated by bisection down to this
// minimal repro (no string-processing builtins needed at all -- `&&`/`||`
// alone, on plain integers, is enough):
//
//   fn f(n: i64) -> str {
//       let mut out: str = ""
//       let mut i: i64 = 0
//       while i < n {
//           if i >= 1 && i <= 3 {       // `||` reproduces identically
//               out = out + "Z"
//           } else {
//               out = out + "Y"
//           }
//           i = i + 1
//       }
//       return out
//   }
//
// Measured NOT required for the crash (each removed independently, repro
// still fires until BOTH the loop and the `&&`/`||` are removed together):
//   - array indexing in either branch (removing it still crashes)
//   - `char_code(substr(..))` as the operand (a plain `i64` param triggers
//     it identically)
//   - asymmetric branches (both branches being a plain string literal still
//     crashes; the FUNCTION VALUES concatenated don't matter, only that a
//     `mut str` local is reassigned in both arms of the if/else)
// Measured REQUIRED (removing any one of these three makes it compile
// clean): (1) the condition is a short-circuit `&&` or `||` compound
// boolean (a single comparison alone does not trigger it), (2) the
// if/else sits inside a `while` loop (the identical if/else outside any
// loop compiles fine), (3) `out = out + <expr>` reassigns a `mut str`
// local declared OUTSIDE the loop in BOTH branches (a loop with the same
// `&&` condition but only `println(..)` in each branch, no str reassign,
// compiles fine).
//
// Likely mechanism (not confirmed by reading the codegen, stated as a
// hypothesis for whoever picks this up): `&&`/`||` short-circuit lowering
// emits a different number of wasm stack values on its "short-circuited"
// path vs its "fully evaluated" path; outside a loop this is invisible,
// but the loop's back-edge requires the SAME stack depth on every
// iteration, and something in the if/else-producing-a-value (the str
// reassignment merge point) interacts badly with that.
//
// This is DISTINCT from the P0 `str == str` packed-handle-vs-content bug
// fixed the same session (kryos_string_eq) -- that was a SILENT wrong
// answer with a clean compile; this is a compile-time ICE, correctly
// caught by the validator, never producing a bad artifact.
//
// Workaround: avoid a short-circuit `&&`/`||` condition around a `mut str`
// reassignment inside a loop on `--backend wasm` -- nest two `if`s instead
// of `&&` (each single-condition `if` compiles fine), or accumulate into
// an `[i64]` byte buffer instead of `str` concatenation and build the
// final string once outside the loop.
//
// kryos run tests/known_failures/wasm_shortcircuit_loop_strcat.kry   -- OK, native
// kryos build --backend wasm tests/known_failures/wasm_shortcircuit_loop_strcat.kry -- FAILS

fn f(n: i64) -> str {
    let mut out: str = ""
    let mut i: i64 = 0
    while i < n {
        if i >= 1 && i <= 3 {
            out = out + "Z"
        } else {
            out = out + "Y"
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

fn main() {
    println(f(5))
}
